menu "Quake"

    config QUAKE_BENCHMARK_SECONDS
        int "Benchmark the first N seconds of the attract-mode demo (0 = off)"
        default 10
        range 0 120
        help
            After boot the game plays demo1 in the background of the menu. Its first
            N seconds (from its second frame) are measured: average fps and a timing
            breakdown of the display pipeline are printed on the console. This needs
            no input and does not change what the game does. A timedemo produces the
            same report when it finishes.

    config QUAKE_MEMBENCH
        bool "Run a memory hierarchy benchmark at boot"
        default n
        help
            Measures load latency and read/write/memcpy bandwidth of the TCM, internal SRAM
            and PSRAM (L1-, L2-resident and uncached sizes) and GDMA copy throughput, once
            before the display starts and once with HDMI scan-out running. Prints MEMBENCH
            lines. Adds a few seconds to boot.

    config QUAKE_SCREENSHOTS
        int "After the benchmark, dump N screenshots on the console (0 = off)"
        default 0
        range 0 10
        help
            For checking the picture without a monitor. After the attract-demo benchmark
            window, every 100th presented frame (8-bit pixels plus palette) is printed as
            hex "SHOT" lines; tools/screenshots.py turns them into PNG files. Printing a
            frame takes a few seconds, during which the game is stalled.

    config QUAKE_PROFILE
        bool "Sample the game core's program counter while benchmarking"
        default n
        help
            During a benchmark window or timedemo, a timer interrupt on core 1 reads
            core 0's current program counter from the ASSIST_DEBUG PC-recording
            register. Nothing runs on core 0, so the game is not slowed down. At the
            end of the window, the sampled PCs are printed as "PROF <pc> <count>"
            lines; tools/profile_report.py maps them to functions.

    config QUAKE_PROFILE_CORE1
        bool "Also sample core 1 (from a timer interrupt on core 0)"
        depends on QUAKE_PROFILE
        default n
        help
            Costs core 0 a little time (about 0.2% at 2 kHz). Core 1 samples are printed
            as "PROF1 <pc> <count>"; use tools/profile_report.py --core 1.

    config QUAKE_PROFILE_SAMPLE_HZ
        int "Sample rate (Hz)"
        depends on QUAKE_PROFILE
        default 2000
        range 100 20000

    config QUAKE_PROFILE_MAX_SAMPLES
        int "Maximum number of samples (4 bytes each, in PSRAM)"
        depends on QUAKE_PROFILE
        default 262144

    config QUAKE_NATIVE_1280x720
        bool "Render 1280x720 natively (no upscaling)"
        depends on BSP_LCD_HDMI_1280x720_60HZ
        default n
        help
            With the 1280x720 HDMI mode, render the game at 1280x720 instead of 512x288 scaled
            up 2.5x by the PPA. 3x the pixels of 640x480, so expect a low frame rate. To fit
            PSRAM, the hunk shrinks to 10 MB and the surface cache to the stock size.

    config QUAKE_L2_AUTOLOAD
        bool "L2 cache hardware autoload on the surface cache and hunk (unsafe)"
        default n
        help
            Prefetches the next cache line on misses and hits in the two regions (vid_null.c).
            Worth ~1.5% fps, but with it on the game crashed on corrupted data 3 times in 11
            minutes, and 0 times in 19 minutes with it off (PERF_LOG.md, stability
            investigation). Off by default.

    config QUAKE_STABILITY_DEBUG
        bool "Stability diagnostics (reset record, stall watchdog, black-frame log)"
        default n
        help
            Prints the reset reason and the previous run's last state at boot, reports
            frame stalls with both cores' sampled program counters, and logs runs of
            black frames. Output lines start with "STAB" (main/stability.c).

endmenu
